sections.s2_technical:
Java lambdas capture the VALUE of local variables. For primitives, this is the actual value. For objects, this is the REFERENCE (memory address). The reference value cannot change (effectively final), but the object the reference points to can be modified through its methods.

```java
// ═══ PRIMITIVE CAPTURE: copies the VALUE ═══
int threshold = 100;
Predicate<Trade> filter = t -> t.getQuantity() > threshold;
// Lambda has a COPY of 100. If threshold could change to 200, lambda still uses 100.
// threshold = 200;  // COMPILE ERROR — effectively final required

// ═══ OBJECT REFERENCE CAPTURE: copies the REFERENCE, object is mutable ═══
List<String> results = new ArrayList<>();  // reference is effectively final
trades.stream().forEach(t -> {
    results.add(t.getSymbol());  // ✅ OK! Reference 'results' hasn't changed.
    // The LIST CONTENTS change — but the REFERENCE to the list is still the same.
});
// results = new ArrayList<>();  // COMPILE ERROR — can't change the REFERENCE!
// results.add("X");             // ✅ OK — changing contents, not reference.
```

The distinction is between the REFERENCE (the variable itself) and the OBJECT (what the variable points to):

```java
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();  // list = reference to ArrayList@0x1234

// Lambda captures: the VALUE of 'list' = 0x1234 (the memory address)
// Lambda can: call list.add() — modifies the object at 0x1234
// Lambda cannot: change list to point to a different ArrayList (0x5678)

Runnable r = () -> {
    list.add("RELIANCE");    // ✅ Mutating the OBJECT — allowed
    // list = new ArrayList<>();  // ❌ Changing the REFERENCE — not allowed
};
```

`this` is also captured as a reference — so instance fields are freely readable and mutable:

```java
class TradeService {
    private int processedCount = 0;  // instance field — NOT a local variable

    void process(List<Trade> trades) {
        trades.forEach(t -> {
            processedCount++;          // ✅ Mutating instance field via 'this'
            // 'this' is captured as a reference. Instance fields accessed through 'this'.
        });
    }
}
```

True closures in other languages capture the VARIABLE BINDING:

```javascript
// JavaScript — TRUE closure: captures the variable binding
let count = 0;
const increment = () => { count++; };  // captures the VARIABLE 'count', not its value
increment();
console.log(count);  // 1 — the SAME variable was modified!

// Java equivalent would need AtomicInteger because Java captures VALUE:
AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
Runnable increment = () -> count.incrementAndGet();
// Reference to AtomicInteger is captured (final). Internal value mutated via method.
```

